<.shell current={:action_bar}>
  <h1>Action bar</h1>
  <p class="lead">The one button for a long list of checked rows.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <.action_bar
      id="action-bar-demo-idle"
      hint="Check members, then change their travel together."
    >
      <.button type="button" variant={:primary}>Change members</.button>
    </.action_bar>
  </div>

  <h3>With rows checked</h3>
  <div class="demo">
    <.action_bar id="action-bar-demo-checked">
      <.button type="button" variant={:primary}>Change 3 members</.button>
    </.action_bar>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    A long list of checked rows with one button, such as an activity's Travel. The bar sits
    under the list and sticks to the foot of the window, so the button stays in view as the
    list scrolls. At the end of the list it sits under it.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A short list, or several buttons for the ticked rows, such as Members' Add to group. They go in a
      <code>.table-actions</code>
      row above the table: see <a href={page_path(:filter_a_list)}>Filter a list</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A form's own Save. It goes at the form's end, as <a href={page_path(:forms)}>Forms</a>
      shows.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One button, which counts who it reaches: Change 3 members.</li>
    <li>
      With nothing checked the hint beside it says what to do first: Check members, then change
      their travel together.
    </li>
    <li>A rule over it and the page's background under it, so rows scroll behind it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.action_bar id="…" hint="…"&gt;</code>
    with the button inside. The hint is optional, and its id is the bar's with <code>-hint</code>. It goes inside the list's form, after the list, so the button submits the ticks.
  </p>
</.shell>
